{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-71268" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the planes $z=1$ and $x=y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71268" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that: \n\n- For the plane $z=1$, a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,0,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x=y$ (or $x-y=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-1,0⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨0,0,1⟩\\times ⟨1,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i+j \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71268" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b175b0ab4ad058714b06a2abaf7cc561e52cfebf3ef81caada55893e769e68f2"} {"question" "q-71268" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71268" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-1,0,0⟩"] ["math" "⟨0,0,1⟩"] ["math" "⟨0,1,-1⟩"] ["math" "⟨1,-1,0⟩"] ["math" "⟨1,1,0⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71268" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨1,1,0⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71251" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y-z+1=0 \\\\ 2x-y+4=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71251" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $y-z+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,-1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x-y+4=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,-1,0⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨0,1,-1⟩\\times ⟨2,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-2j-2k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71251" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b175b0ab4ad058714b06a2abaf7cc561e52cfebf3ef81caada55893e769e68f2"} {"question" "q-71251" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71251" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-1,-2,-2⟩"] ["math" "⟨-3,-2,1⟩"] ["math" "⟨0,1,-1⟩"] ["math" "⟨2,-1,0⟩"] ["math" "⟨2,-1,4⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71251" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨-1,-2,-2⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-129170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3x+4z-11=0 \\\\ x-2y+31=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-129170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $-3x+4z-11=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨-3,0,4⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-2y+31=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-2,0⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,4⟩\\times ⟨1,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =8i+4j+6k \\\\  & =⟨8,4,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-129170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b175b0ab4ad058714b06a2abaf7cc561e52cfebf3ef81caada55893e769e68f2"} {"question" "q-129170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-129170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨4,0,3⟩"] ["math" "⟨4,2,0⟩"] ["math" "⟨4,4,6⟩"] ["math" "⟨4,8,6⟩"] ["math" "⟨8,4,6⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-129170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨8,4,6⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71260" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2x+3z=0 \\\\ 4y+2z-1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71260" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $-2x+3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨-2,0,3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $4y+2z-1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,4,2⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨-2,0,3⟩\\times ⟨0,4,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-12i+4j-8k \\\\  & =⟨-12,4,-8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71260" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b175b0ab4ad058714b06a2abaf7cc561e52cfebf3ef81caada55893e769e68f2"} {"question" "q-71260" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71260" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-12,4,-8⟩"] ["math" "⟨-2,0,3⟩"] ["math" "⟨-2,3,0⟩"] ["math" "⟨0,4,2⟩"] ["math" "⟨4,2,-1⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71260" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨-12,4,-8⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71266" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5x-4y=0 \\\\ 4y-5z=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71266" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $5x-4y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨5,-4,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $4y-5z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,4,-5⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨5,-4,0⟩\\times ⟨0,4,-5⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -5\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =20i+25j+20k \\\\  & =⟨20,25,20⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71266" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b175b0ab4ad058714b06a2abaf7cc561e52cfebf3ef81caada55893e769e68f2"} {"question" "q-71266" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71266" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨0,0,-9⟩"] ["math" "⟨0,1,-5⟩"] ["math" "⟨20,25,20⟩"] ["math" "⟨4,-5,0⟩"] ["math" "⟨5,-4,0⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71266" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨20,25,20⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intersection of the planes $x+2y-4z+6=0$ and $x-4y+2z=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨0,{a}_{2},{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{2}+{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $x=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n0-4y+2z=0\\,⟹\\,z=2y,\n$$\n\nand substituting $x=0$ and $z=2y$ into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y-4z+6 & =0 \\\\ 0+2y-4(2y)+6 & =0 \\\\ -6y & =-6 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $z=2y=2$ and $a=⟨0,1,2⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+2y-4z+6=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,2,-4⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-4y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-4,2⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,2,-4⟩\\times ⟨1,-4,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-12i-6j-6k \\\\  & =⟨-12,-6,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n \n\n$$\nb=-\\frac{1}{6}({n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2})=⟨2,1,1⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨0,1,2⟩+t⟨2,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=1+2=3,$ and $b=⟨2,1,1⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b175b0ab4ad058714b06a2abaf7cc561e52cfebf3ef81caada55893e769e68f2"} {"question" "q-71447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=-2,\\,b=⟨2,1,2⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=-3,\\,b=⟨2,1,3⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=2,\\,b=⟨2,2,1⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=3,\\,b=⟨2,1,1⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=6,\\,b=⟨1,3,6⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=3,\\,b=⟨2,1,1⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intersection of the planes $-x+y-2z=0$ and $-x-2y+z-3=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n-x+y-2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,x=y,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $x=y$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x-2y+z-3 & =0 \\\\ -y-2y+0-3 & =0 \\\\ -3y & =3 \\\\ y & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=y=-1$ and $a=⟨-1,-1,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $-x+y-2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨-1,1,-2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $-x-2y+z-3=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨-1,-2,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨-1,1,-2⟩\\times ⟨-1,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-3i+3j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n\n$$\nb=\\frac{1}{3}({n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2})=⟨-1,1,1⟩.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨-1,-1,0⟩+t⟨-1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-1-1=-2,$ and $b=⟨-1,1,1⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b175b0ab4ad058714b06a2abaf7cc561e52cfebf3ef81caada55893e769e68f2"} {"question" "q-71187" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71187" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-1,\\,b=⟨-1,1,1⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-1,\\,b=⟨1,0,1⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-2,\\,b=⟨-1,1,1⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-2,\\,b=⟨1,0,1⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-5,\\,b=⟨1,0,1⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71187" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-2,\\,b=⟨-1,1,1⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-129202" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intersection of the planes $3x-2y-18=0$ and $2y-3z+18=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},0,{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}-{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-129202" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $y=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives \n\n$$\n3x-0-18=0\\,⟹\\,x=6,\n$$\n\nand substituting $y=0$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n0-3z+18=0\\,⟹\\,z=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=⟨6,0,6⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $3x-2y-18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,-2,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2y-3z+18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,0⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =6i+9j+6k \\\\  & =⟨6,9,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨6,0,6⟩+t⟨6,9,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=6-6=0,$ and $b=⟨6,9,6⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-129202" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b175b0ab4ad058714b06a2abaf7cc561e52cfebf3ef81caada55893e769e68f2"} {"question" "q-129202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-129202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=-3,\\,b=⟨9,6,6⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=0,\\,b=⟨3,9,3⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=0,\\,b=⟨6,9,6⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=3,\\,b=⟨6,3,6⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=6,\\,b=⟨6,6,3⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-129202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=0,\\,b=⟨6,9,6⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-129224" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intersection of the planes $2x-y-3z-8=0$ and $x+2y-3z-4=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-129224" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n2x-y-3(0)-8=0\\,⟹\\,y=2x-8,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $y=2x-8$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2(2x-8)-0-4 & =0 \\\\ x+4x-16-4 & =0 \\\\ 5x & =20 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=2(4)-8=0$ and $a=⟨4,0,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x-y-3z-8=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-1,-3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+2y-3z-4=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-1,-3⟩\\times ⟨1,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =9i+3j+5k \\\\  & =⟨9,3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨4,0,0⟩+t⟨9,3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=4+0=4,$ and $b=⟨9,3,5⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-129224" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b175b0ab4ad058714b06a2abaf7cc561e52cfebf3ef81caada55893e769e68f2"} {"question" "q-129224" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-129224" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-2,\\,b=⟨6,3,5⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-4,\\,b=⟨9,5,3⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=2,\\,b=⟨3,9,5⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=4,\\,b=⟨9,3,5⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=8,\\,b=⟨5,3,9⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-129224" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=4,\\,b=⟨9,3,5⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intersection of the planes $2x-y+6=0$ and $y+2z-2=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},0,{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}-{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $y=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives \n\n$$\n2x-0+6=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3,\n$$\n\nand substituting $y=0$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n0+2z-2=0\\,⟹\\,z=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=⟨-3,0,1⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x-y+6=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $y+2z-2=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,2⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-2i-4j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-4,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨-3,0,1⟩+t⟨-2,-4,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=-3-1=-4,$ and $b=⟨-2,-4,2⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b175b0ab4ad058714b06a2abaf7cc561e52cfebf3ef81caada55893e769e68f2"} {"question" "q-71347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=-2,\\,b=⟨-4,2,2⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=-4,\\,b=⟨-2,-4,2⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=2,\\,b=⟨-4,2,2⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=3,\\,b=⟨-2,-4,2⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=3,\\,b=⟨-4,2,2⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=-4,\\,b=⟨-2,-4,2⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71468" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the two planes $2x-4y+6z+2=0$ and $-x+2y-3z=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n- The planes intersect on a straight line.\n- The planes are coincident." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71468" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $2x-4y+6z+2=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-4,6⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $-x+2y-3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨-1,2,-3⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-4,6⟩\\times ⟨-1,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =0i+0j+0k \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}=0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are parallel. Hence, the corresponding planes are also parallel. So, the planes can't intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed,  the second plane passes through the origin  $O(0,0,0)$ but the first plane doesn't: \n\n$$\n2(0)-4(0)+6(0)+2=2\\ne 0\n$$\n\n So, the planes can't be coincident.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71468" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b175b0ab4ad058714b06a2abaf7cc561e52cfebf3ef81caada55893e769e68f2"} {"question" "q-71468" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71468" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71468" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71456" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the two planes $x-3y+z=0$ and $2x-y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨1,5,2⟩.$\n- The planes intersect on a line.\n- The planes are coincident." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71456" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-3y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-3,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x-y+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,-1,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-3,1⟩\\times ⟨2,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i+2j+5k \\\\  & =⟨1,2,5⟩\\ne ⟨1,5,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes cannot be coincident.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71456" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b175b0ab4ad058714b06a2abaf7cc561e52cfebf3ef81caada55893e769e68f2"} {"question" "q-71456" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71456" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71456" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the two planes $3x-y+z=0$ and $x-z+4=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n- The planes are parallel.\n- The planes intersect on a line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $3x-y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,-1,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-z+4=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨3,-1,1⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i+4j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,4,1⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b175b0ab4ad058714b06a2abaf7cc561e52cfebf3ef81caada55893e769e68f2"} {"question" "q-71110" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71110" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-71110" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the two planes $2x-y+3z=0$ and $5x-2y+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨7,15,2⟩.$\n- The planes intersect on a line.\n- The planes are coincident." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $2x-y+3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-1,3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $5x-2y+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨5,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-1,3⟩\\times ⟨5,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =6i+15j+k \\\\  & =⟨6,15,1⟩\\ne ⟨7,15,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes cannot be coincident.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b175b0ab4ad058714b06a2abaf7cc561e52cfebf3ef81caada55893e769e68f2"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-129239" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the two planes $2x-5y+z+1=0$ and $3x-z+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n- The planes are parallel.\n- The planes intersect on a line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-129239" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $2x-5y+z+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-5,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x-z+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,0,-1⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-5,1⟩\\times ⟨3,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =5i+5j+15k \\\\  & =⟨5,5,15⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-129239" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b175b0ab4ad058714b06a2abaf7cc561e52cfebf3ef81caada55893e769e68f2"} {"question" "q-129239" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-129239" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "q-129239" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fec984959e663e4a7e488e0598b7ea4e8315da08f4c1cc678a3774fa525088cc"} {"question" "e-5920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+4y+5=0 \\\\ 3y-2z-7=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/example-5920.json" "file_sha256" "bed14be223a6c812c5876091eb7a34b1f61a32e26594f9fae33a50ea5de21577"} {"question" "e-5920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x+4y +5= 0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 2,4,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $3y-2z-7= 0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle 0,3,-2 \\rangle.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,4,0⟩\\times ⟨0,3,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\,i & \\,j & \\,k\\, \\\\ \\,2 & \\,4 & \\,0\\, \\\\ \\,0 & \\,3 & \\,\\,\\,-2\\,\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-8i+4j+6k \\\\  & =⟨-8,4,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/example-5920.json" "file_sha256" "bed14be223a6c812c5876091eb7a34b1f61a32e26594f9fae33a50ea5de21577"} {"question" "e-5917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intersection of the planes $x+2y+4z=0$ and $2x+4y+2z+18=0$ is given by the line \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 0, a_2, a_3\\rangle + t \\mathbf b, \\quad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nCalculate $a_2+a_3,$ and find a possible direction vector $\\mathbf b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/example-5917.json" "file_sha256" "54a524d1db17c39bc446363252079dd3624c71e6a1ccee49fb20b1a7421a2edf"} {"question" "e-5917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $x=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives   \n\n$$\n0+2y+4z=0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad y=-2z,\n$$\n\nand substituting $x=0$ and $y=-2z$ into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4y+2z+18 & =0 \\\\ 2(0)+4(-2z)+2z+18 & =0 \\\\ -6z & =-18 \\\\ z & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-2z=-6$ and $\\mathbf a = \\langle 0,-6,3\\rangle$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+2y+4z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 1,2,4 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $2x+4y+2z+18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle 2,4,2 \\rangle.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,2,4⟩\\times ⟨2,4,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-12i+6j \\\\  & =⟨-12,6,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} =\\dfrac{1}{6}(\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2) = \\langle -2, 1, 0 \\rangle.\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨0,-6,3⟩+t⟨-2,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, $a_2 +a_3 = -6+3 = -3,$ and $\\mathbf b = \\langle  -2,1,0\\rangle.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/example-5917.json" "file_sha256" "54a524d1db17c39bc446363252079dd3624c71e6a1ccee49fb20b1a7421a2edf"} {"question" "e-5921" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the two planes $4x-2y=0$ and $-2x+y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $\\mathbf{0}.$\n\n- The planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\n- The planes intersect on a straight line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/example-5921.json" "file_sha256" "30e47e1674a220532fee83982ff9c230d8cace64a10c6c475367ef03a9dfd222"} {"question" "e-5921" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that: \n\n- For the plane $4x-2y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 4,-2,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $-2x+y+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle -2,1,0 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨4,-2,0⟩\\times ⟨-2,1,0⟩ \\\\ n & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2 =\\mathbf{0}$, the vectors $\\mathbf{n}_1$ and $\\mathbf{n}_2$ are parallel. However,  the first plane passes through the origin  $O(0,0,0)$ but the second plane doesn't: \n\n$$\n-2(0)+(0)+1=1 \\neq 0\n$$\n\n So, the planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the planes are parallel but not coincident, they cannot intersect each other.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/example-5921.json" "file_sha256" "30e47e1674a220532fee83982ff9c230d8cace64a10c6c475367ef03a9dfd222"}] "usage" {"e-5917" [] "e-5921" [] "q-71110" [] "q-71187" [] "e-5920" []} "basis" 1463 "retractions" [[17592186148147 :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $-x + y - 2z = 0$ and $-x - 2y + z - 3 = 0$ is given by the line\n\n$r(t) = ⟨a_{1}, a_{2}, 0⟩ + tb, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$.\n\nCalculate $a_{1} + a_{2}$, and find a possible direction vector $b$."] [17592186148144 :question/problem "Given the two planes $3x - y + z = 0$ and $x - z + 4 = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0$.\n2. The planes are parallel.\n3. The planes intersect on a line."] [17592186147285 :question/problem "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n${2x + 4y + 5 = 0; 3y - 2z - 7 = 0$"] [17592186147285 :question/worked-solution "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x+4y +5= 0$, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 2,4,0 \\rangle$.\n- For the plane $3y-2z-7= 0$, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle 0,3,-2 \\rangle$.\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= n_{1} \\cdot n_{2} \\\\\n&= ⟨2, 4, 0⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 3,-2⟩ \\\\\n&= i\\begin{vmatrix}j & k \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}0 \\\\\n&= 0 \\mid 3 \\mid - 2 \\\\\n&=-8i + 4j + 6k \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 8, 4, 6⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186147284 :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $x+2y+4z=0$ and $2x+4y+2z+18=0$ is given by the line\n\n$\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 0, a_2, a_3\\rangle + t \\mathbf b, \\quad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nCalculate $a_2+a_3$, and find a possible direction vector $\\mathbf b$."] [17592186147284 :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $x=0$. Substituting this into the first equation gives\n\n$$\n0+2y+4z=0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad y=-2z\n$$\n\nand substituting $x=0$ and $y=-2z$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + 4y + 2z + 18 &= 0 \\\\\n2(0) + 4(-2z) + 2z + 18 &= 0 \\\\\n- 6z &= -18 \\\\\nz &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-2z=-6$ and\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a = \\langle 0,-6,3\\rangle\n$$\n\nis a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+2y+4z=0$, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 1,2,4 \\rangle$.\n- For the plane $2x+4y+2z+18=0$, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle 2,4,2 \\rangle$.\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn_{1} \\cdot n_{2} &= ⟨1, 2, 4⟩ \\cdot ⟨2, 4, 2⟩ \\\\\n&= i\\begin{vmatrix}j & k \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}4 \\\\\n&= 2 \\mid 4 \\mid 2 \\\\\n&=-12i + 6j \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 12, 6, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} =\\dfrac{1}{6}(\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2) = \\langle -2, 1, 0 \\rangle\n$$\n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= a + tb \\\\\nr(t) &= ⟨0,-6, 3⟩ + t⟨ - 2, 1, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\na_2 +a_3 = -6+3 = -3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf b = \\langle -2,1,0\\rangle\n$$"] [17592186147286 :question/problem "Given the two planes $4x-2y=0$ and $-2x+y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $\\mathbf{0}$.\n2. The planes are parallel but not coincident.\n3. The planes intersect on a straight line."] [17592186147286 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $4x-2y=0$, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 4,-2,0 \\rangle$.\n- For the plane $-2x+y+1=0$, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle -2,1,0 \\rangle$.\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n$n_{1} \\cdot n_{2}|= ⟨4,-2, 0⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - 2, 1, 0⟩; n|= i\\begin{vmatrix}j & k \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}0; - 2 \\mid 1 \\mid 0; = 0$.\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2 =\\mathbf{0}$, the vectors $\\mathbf{n}_1$ and $\\mathbf{n}_2$ are parallel. However, the first plane passes through the origin $O(0,0,0)$ but the second plane doesn't: $-2(0)+(0)+1=1 \\neq 0$ So, the planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the planes are parallel but not coincident, they cannot intersect each other.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186146876 17592186146877 17592186146878 17592186146879 17592186146880 17592186146894 17592186146895 17592186146896 17592186146897 17592186146898] "immutable_fields" [[17592186146875 :answer-field/correct] [17592186146875 :answer-field/id] [17592186146875 :answer-field/key] [17592186146875 :answer-field/type] [17592186146893 :answer-field/correct] [17592186146893 :answer-field/id] [17592186146893 :answer-field/key] [17592186146893 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
